  {
    "verifyText": {
      "id": "//div[@id='lf_inline_modal_body']/div/p",
      "value": "If you need to finance a major purchase, home repair/upgrades, or unexpected expenses, your options include home equity loans and lines of credit, personal loans, and credit cards. Each option has distinct benefits and drawbacks."
    }
  },
  {
    "verifyText": {
      "id": "//div[@id='lf_inline_modal_body']/div/dl/dt",
      "value": "Home equity loans and lines of credit"
    }
  },
  {
    "verifyText": {
      "id": "//div[@id='lf_inline_modal_body']/div/dl/dd/dl/dt",
      "value": "Pros"
    }
  },
  {
    "verifyText": {
      "id": "//div[@id='lf_inline_modal_body']/div/dl/dd/dl/dd",
      "value": "Interest rates on home equity financing are typically lower than credit cards or personal loans, which could mean lower monthly payments. Home equity interest payments are generally tax deductible and have higher credit limits, giving you access to more funds. With home equity lines of credit or HELOCs, you have the flexibility of accessing your variable-rate line of credit during the draw period, paying interest and principal only on the funds you use."
    }
  },
  {
    "verifyText": {
      "id": "//div[@id='lf_inline_modal_body']/div/dl/dd/dl/dt[2]",
      "value": "Cons"
    }
  },
  {
    "verifyText": {
      "id": "//div[@id='lf_inline_modal_body']/div/dl/dd/dl/dd[2]",
      "value": "If you are unable to keep up with your monthly payments, you may be forced out of your home, although foreclosure laws concerning timing and procedures vary by state. Your HELOC may also be frozen by the lender if your home dramatically loses value or it reasonably believes you won't be able to repay the loan. Adjustable-rate HELOCs also carry rate risk, meaning that if rates rise, your monthly payment will rise as well. Be sure that you have the financial resources to weather the possibility of rising interest rates."
    }
  },
  {
    "verifyText": {
      "id": "//div[@id='lf_inline_modal_body']/div/dl/dt[2]",
      "value": "Credit cards"
    }
  },
  {
    "verifyText": {
      "id": "//div[@id='lf_inline_modal_body']/div/dl/dd[2]/dl/dt",
      "value": "Pros"
    }
  },
  {
    "verifyText": {
      "id": "//div[@id='lf_inline_modal_body']/div/dl/dd[2]/dl/dd",
      "value": "Whether it's points, miles or cash back, using a rewards credit card for a major purchase can put extra money in your pockets. Credit cards that offer a 0 percent introductory APR on purchases for a period of time (generally from 12 to 18 months) can serve as an interest-free loans provided you pay the balance off by the time the intro period ends."
    }
  },
  {
    "verifyText": {
      "id": "//div[@id='lf_inline_modal_body']/div/dl/dd[2]/dl/dt[2]",
      "value": "Cons"
    }
  },
  {
    "verifyText": {
      "id": "//div[@id='lf_inline_modal_body']/div/dl/dd[2]/dl/dd[2]",
      "value": "If you can't pay off the balance quickly, you could end up paying a lot more for your purchase than you ever planned. Compound interest on a high balance grows quickly. In addition, credit cards can have a negative impact on your credit score if you use a high percentage of the total credit limit on the card."
    }
  },
  {
    "verifyText": {
      "id": "//div[@id='lf_inline_modal_body']/div/dl/dt[3]",
      "value": "Personal loans"
    }
  },
  {
    "verifyText": {
